1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,930
Welcome to the last video in the
series. You have your story.

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:07,140
Now let’s make sure people
can hear it clearly.

3
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:09,440
Sound quality changes
whether people believe you.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:14,160
In a 2018 study, people heard the
same scientists give the same talks.

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,780
When the audio was poor,

6
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:21,760
listeners rated the speakers less likeable
and their research less important.

7
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:26,780
Same words, same ideas, judged
differently just because of the sound.

8
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:28,140
Start with the room.

9
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:29,610
An empty room echoes.

10
00:00:29,610 --> 00:00:35,460
Hard walls and bare floors throw your voice
back at the microphone, and it ends up sounding

11
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:37,080
like you’re in a hallway.

12
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:40,990
So choose a small room with
soft things in it. Curtains,

13
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:43,760
a bed and a rug soak up the echo.

14
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,720
A bedroom is ideal. Avoid kitchens,
bathrooms and empty rooms.

15
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:52,890
Then switch off the noise. Turn
off fans and air conditioning,

16
00:00:52,890 --> 00:00:56,790
close the windows and put
your phone on silent.

17
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:03,040
Here’s a quick check: clap once. If you
hear it ring afterwards, the room echoes.

18
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:05,360
Add a blanket or move rooms.

19
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:07,400
Next, the microphone.

20
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:13,480
Wired earbuds with a mic are best for most
people. They keep the mic close and steady.

21
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:18,000
Your phone works well too,
held 15–20 cm from your mouth.

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,310
Your computer’s own mic only as a last resort
because it picks up the room and the keyboard.

23
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:27,520
For every full take, do the 10-second test.

24
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,120
Record 10 seconds, then
play it back on headphones.

25
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:35,020
Listen for four things: echo,
hum from a fan or fridge,

26
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:39,200
distortion when you’re too
loud, and sounding far away.

27
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,560
Fix one, then test again.

28
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:47,720
Now, how you speak. Use bullet points, not a
script, because reading flattens your voice.

29
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:52,780
Slow down. Two minutes at an
easy pace is about 260 words.

30
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:57,520
Pause between beats, and
record one take per beat.

31
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:01,080
So if you stumble, you only redo that part.

32
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:04,500
Before you press record,
turn off notifications,

33
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:09,520
close other tabs and zoom your browser
in so it’s readable on a phone.

34
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:15,720
If you’re on camera, raise it to eye level and
keep the light in front of you, not behind.

35
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:19,310
To finish, no music under your voice.

36
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:25,120
Turn on captions and fix any names they
got wrong, and name your file exactly.

37
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:28,560
First name, last name, project name, demo.

38
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:34,000
Your turn. One, do the clap test in
two rooms and choose the better one.

39
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,800
Two, record one full take. Three,

40
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:41,080
listen back on headphones and
fix the problem you hear most.

41
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:47,120
Four, record it again. Worksheet 5 in the
handbook is your recording checklist.

42
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:48,990
That’s all four videos.

43
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:55,080
Tell it as a story, make it believable, win
the first 15 seconds and record it well.

44
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:58,600
Now go and tell your story.
